---
/**
 * `/memory` - the archive.
 *
 * THIS PAGE NOW SHOWS REAL ROWS, and until 2026-08-08 it did not. It carried a notice saying the
 * browsable archive was not built, because no endpoint listed memories, and racking invented strips
 * on the one page whose job is to be checkable would have been the worst lie on the site. `GET
 * /memories` exists now, so the notice is gone and the rows are live.
 *
 * THREE STATEMENTS HAD TO CHANGE TOGETHER and they are listed here because missing one would leave
 * the page contradicting itself: the notice bay, the third source strip row that read UNKNOWN, and
 * the colophon explaining why the archive's contents were absent. A page that racks live rows while
 * still printing "there is no list endpoint yet" in its own footer is worse than either version.
 *
 * WHAT IS STILL AN ILLUSTRATION is the eviction bay, and it says so. Nothing here has ever been
 * evicted, because eviction runs on a schedule this demo does not run, so the grace window is
 * described from `policy.ts` rather than shown. Every number in it is imported, so the description
 * cannot drift from the code it describes.
 */
import { DEFAULT_POLICY, MS_PER_DAY } from '@throughline/memory';
import '../styles/archive.css';
import Archive from '../islands/Archive.tsx';
import Board from '../layouts/Board.astro';
import { API_BASE } from '../scripts/config.ts';
import { articleSchema } from '../scripts/schema.ts';
import type { SourceRow } from '../scripts/types.ts';

const site = Astro.site?.href ?? Astro.url.origin;
const policy = DEFAULT_POLICY;
const graceHours = policy.graceWindowMs / (MS_PER_DAY / 24);

const sources: readonly SourceRow[] = [
  { claim: 'Half-lives, the grace window and the similarity floor, imported from policy.ts', trust: 'MEASURED' },
  // THE CLAIM IS ABOUT PROVENANCE AND SAYS SO, because this strip is static HTML and cannot report a
  // runtime outcome. A review pointed out that the first wording ("the rows in bay 1, read from the
  // live archive") stamps MEASURED on rows that are absent before hydration and stay absent when the
  // API is unreachable, and that the row it replaced was the hedge which made the strip safe under
  // every outcome. So the claim now names where bay 1's rows come from and hands the presence
  // question to the receipt, which is the only thing that can answer it.
  {
    claim: 'Bay 1 is read from GET /memories; whether the listing ran is on its receipt, not here',
    trust: 'MEASURED',
  },
  // Still UNKNOWN, deliberately, and it is a different claim from the row above. Nothing on this
  // page has been evicted, because eviction runs on a schedule this demo does not run.
  { claim: 'What the eviction sweep would refuse, which has never run here', trust: 'UNKNOWN' },
];
---

<Board
  title="Archive - Throughline"
  heading="The archive: how a memory row is structured and what may not be deleted"
  description="How the memory archive is structured: typed rows, supersede chains that keep the old row queryable, tombstones instead of deletions, and a grace window nothing can be evicted inside."
  designator={['TLN-01 / Archive', 'Supersede and tombstone', 'Never overwrite']}
  sources={sources}
  colophon="The rows in bay 1 are read from the live archive, with the listing's own receipt printed above them, so an empty rack and a failed query never look alike. The eviction bay is still a description: nothing here has been evicted, because that sweep does not run in this demo."
  jsonLd={articleSchema(site, '/memory/', 'How the Throughline archive is structured')}
>
  {/* No wrapping heading: the island renders its own `bayhead` with the h2, the same way
      `StatusBoard` does on /status. A second heading here would either duplicate it visibly or need
      a hiding class, and `sr-only` exists for text that has no visible counterpart, not for one that
      does. */}
  <section class="bay">
    <Archive client:load apiBase={API_BASE} />
  </section>

  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="chain">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Bay 2</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="chain">How to read a supersede chain above</h2>
      <p class="baynote">
        Contradicting a fact does not overwrite it. It closes the old row's validity interval and
        links the two, so "why did you tell me that in June" is a question the archive can answer.
        The replacement keeps its kind colour and reads <em>Current</em>; the row it replaced is
        dimmed, stamped <em>Superseded</em>, and still carries the id of what replaced it. Both are
        in the rack above if the workspace holds a chain, because neither is filtered out.
      </p>
      <p class="baynote">
        A tombstone is the other one. An evicted row is not deleted: it takes the grey holder, keeps
        every field it had, and gains the date it was removed and the reason. That is why this archive
        gets longer rather than shorter, and it is the thing a plain vector store cannot show you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="eviction">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Bay 3</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="eviction">What eviction may not touch</h2>
      <p class="baynote">
        Eviction is age and value based with a grace window, and it reports what it refused as well
        as what it removed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rack">
      <div><span class="tag">Refused &middot; inside the {graceHours} h grace window</span></div>
      <div><span class="tag">Refused &middot; write with no provenance, rejected at the boundary</span></div>
      <p class="baynote">
        Nothing younger than {graceHours} hours can be evicted at any score, because the incident
        that just happened is the one most likely to recur within the hour and it has no usage yet.
        An evicted row leaves a tombstone, so the archive stays auditable rather than becoming
        shorter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bay" aria-labelledby="kinds">
    <div class="bayhead">
      <span class="bayno">Bay 4</span>
      <h2 class="baylabel" id="kinds">What a row carries</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Content and embedding, the kind, provenance naming who or what asserted it and out of which
        incident, confirmation and contradiction counts, the interval over which it is claimed to
        hold, a pointer to whatever superseded it, and the instant before which it cannot be
        evicted. A write with no provenance is rejected at the boundary rather than warned about,
        because a memory you cannot attribute is a rumour.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ranking is deterministic. Similarity, time decay and confirmation combine into a single
        score computed in code, weighted {policy.weights.similarity} similarity, {policy.weights.freshness} freshness
        and {policy.weights.confirmation} confirmation. Contradiction is the one that does not add:
        it SUBTRACTS at {policy.weights.contradiction}, which is more than confirmation adds, so a
        memory that has been argued with sinks faster than agreement can lift it. The language model
        writes the narrative around a result and never produces the number that ordered it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Board>
